ntification you can only assess  whether your sample is above the limit  of quantification so you can say oh yeah  I can quantify this but you just you  can't say but and I can and fill this  low right but this is still important  information and useful information  so another way to quantify is actually  by doing just a dilution series of your  sample in in plasma and so we actually  did this for an alzheimer's disease  study that we were working on  Alzheimer's disease is a really huge  health crisis right now it's estimated  to have a 1.1 trillion dollar health  care cost in 2050 and it's known to be  based in protein so looking at April  lipoprotein II there are three different  variants that are their normal high risk  or protective for Alzheimer's disease  and so what we actually did here were  looking for different proteins in  Alzheimer's disease and we just took our  human sample and diluted it up in  chicken okay so our one hundred percent  concentration our human sample our  lowest concentration is chicken and we  did do our two different dilution series  right to make sure that we take care of  any systemic errors and what you can see  is when you do this you get nice clear  results so this is for the protein the  peptide ihw from co 3 ok you can also  use healthy disease pools so for example  if i if the chicken serum didn't work I  could use the Alzheimer's disease sample  and then a healthy sample and I and do  delusions of the two pools to  demonstrate that the measurement is  linear between the two so for example I  can take 100% healthy down to 100%  Alzheimer's and so if we show this data  safer apoe4 some mocked up data what you  would see is that it's linear in between  the healthy and diseased patients  so with that I just want to kind of  summarize the important things about  targeted method development strategies  that we've talked about today so things  to avoid our predictive algorithms data  repositories and DDA data these don't  accuratel