e to measure for right so  your sensitivity is going to be  proportional to the amount of time you  measure a transition and so I I like a  minimum transition era  sorry dwell time of about 10  milliseconds obviously going higher is  better but it says I found it to be kind  of the balance so with that said as my  dwell time is going to be 10  milliseconds on most triple quads  probably 450 to 500 concurrent  transitions you could measure at a  single time thank you  next one is do you have to do such long  digestion or can you add more trypsin  and digest for a shorter period of time  yeah so this is actually a really good  point I didn't talk much about it one of  the slow things about a lot of what I do  and you actually see in the resources I  put up there is that I do an 18-hour  digestion for a lot of experiments so  one thing is that a lot of my work was  done in plasma which is notoriously  difficult to digest due to protease  inhibitors  and so I'm actually using a ton of  trypsin in there but you definitely can  add more trypsin especially if you're  using the cheaper like the cheaper grade  trypsin not pretty omics grade trypsin  you can add more in and one thing I'd  actually suggest doing if you're going  to do that if you add in the trypsin let  it go for 30 minutes to 2 hours and then  do another spike of trypsin thank you  looks like we have time for one more  question why do you use both peptides  and proteins in your internal standard  yeah so this is a really good question  so both peptides and proteins in the  internal standard are because they bring  different things to the table so  peptides you know you should see right  so if you don't see your peptide  internal standard there's probably  something wrong with your setup or your  sampler like your your sample itself say  you're not picking up enough sample to  actually analyze or your nano LC tip has  gone bad or something like that but if  you can see your peptide internal  standard and not y